## Part 3. The subscription list

### 3.1 Definition

Every incident carries a subscription list. The subscription list is the set of roles and units that receive notifications for that incident, and it is the recipient set for all incident paging under this SOP.

### 3.2 Qualification

A role or unit is added to the subscription list when any trigger configured for it is present on the incident. Where more than one trigger applies to the same role, the role is subscribed once. Triggers are evaluated at open and again whenever the incident changes: when the Type is refined or corrected, a Tag is added, the Agency Status changes, a unit is attached, a condition becomes true, the Outcome is set, or the Supervisor adds a subscriber.

### 3.3 Notifiable events

Every member of the subscription list receives every notifiable event for the incident. There is one subscription list and one feed; roles are not tiered.

Decision (N. Wood, 30 Aug 2026): one group, one feed. If an incident is serious enough for the COO and CEO to be subscribed, they receive everything.

Notifiable events:

1. Incident opened
2. Incident Type refined or corrected
3. Tag added
4. Agency Status changed
5. Unit attached or released
6. Protracted condition met
7. Incident Outcome set
8. Incident closed
9. Supervisor broadcast (a message sent by the LSV Comms Supervisor to the subscription list)

A role added to the subscription list after open receives, as its first notification, a summary of the incident as it stands.

### 3.4 Clearing

Any role or unit on the subscription list may be cleared at any time by LSV Comms. A cleared role or unit receives no further notifications for that incident unless re-added. Clearing is recorded in the incident chronology with the time and the operator. Clearing does not alter the incident's Type, Tags, Status or Outcome.

Decision (N. Wood, 30 Aug 2026): subscription is not monotonic; any unit can be cleared at any time.

### 3.5 Worked examples

1. **Rescue with resuscitation.** A swimmer is retrieved from a rip and arrests on the beach. Type: Search And Rescue > Rescue > Person > Water (subscribes SUP, ARO, SDO). Tag Resuscitation added (subscribes WDO, SAC, SMO, LL, COO, CEO). Subscription list: all nine roles. The Type is not changed (v0.9 Rule 1); the Tag carries the escalation.
2. **Activation that becomes a rescue.** A Rescue Equipment Activation is opened (SUP, ARO, SDO). A rescue follows. The Type stays as the activation (v0.9 Rule 4); Tag Rescue Completed is added and the Action Log records the rescue. No new subscribers unless a further Tag or Outcome applies.
3. **Medical opened Not Final, then refined.** An incident is opened as Medical while the presenting problem is assessed (subscribes SUP). It is refined to Chest Pain (adds ARO, SDO). Refinement adds; nothing is removed.
4. **Tag added, then removed.** Tag Member Injury is added on a report that a member was hurt (subscribes WDO and the executive roles). The report proves wrong and the Tag is removed. The subscribers remain on the list until LSV Comms clears them. If LSV Comms clears the executive roles, the chronology records the clearing.
5. **Fatality.** A Rescue ends in a death. Outcome Fatality is set as soon as it is confirmed, during the response. The Outcome rule subscribes any role not already on the list (WDO, SAC, SMO, LL, COO, CEO), each receives the incident summary, and every subscriber receives each subsequent event through to closure.
6. **Search becomes protracted.** A Search > Person > Water reaches 60 minutes in Responding without Under Control. The Protracted rule subscribes SAC, SMO, LL, COO, CEO. They receive the incident summary, then every subsequent event.

